<p>Iranian strikes hit Qatar’s Ras Laffan Industrial City and oil facilities in Kuwait after Israel struck Iran’s South Pars gas field, widening the Israel-Iran war to energy infrastructure across the Gulf. Qatar said damage at Ras Laffan knocked out nearly a fifth of its liquefied natural gas export capacity, while multiple outlets reported that two production trains with a combined annual capacity of 12.8 million tonnes were damaged. The attacks rattled investors and pushed fuel markets higher because Ras Laffan is the world’s largest LNG export complex, and the Strait of Hormuz has become nearly impassable during the conflict, threatening a crucial route for global energy shipments. Gulf states that have often tried to stay outside the confrontation now face direct pressure, even as Qatar’s broader role as a regional mediator gives diplomats another reason to prevent further escalation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/fIJykL9lyfA1LHuvA5sszyzkFiEzVYVjPxU8EZTmIgRgqFkUFHE8uiTfnueDSfJwUAaYYPGhpK51ZvWX-ZtBnBgIS-ya72b0wGS1741RGXgndNWf5YE1a-DczKzBP7cQQCFU1gbO6c0r_8iXSmyA9mbH3FMOxHZMuBR381ARaeDvG_e8qAxQ2egmMyZIuhobSSXSHgHRdyqoqkyZcBycLuMveYbh_svC' alt='Qatar blamed Iran for an attack that damaged the Ras Laffan terminal, the world’s largest liquefied natural gas facility.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Market shock: The strikes pushed global stock markets lower as traders weighed damage at the world’s biggest LNG export complex and the risk of wider shipping disruption across the Gulf.</li><li>Duration risk: Reporting suggested the hit to Ras Laffan could have effects lasting years if repairs move slowly, turning a retaliatory strike into a structural supply problem rather than a brief outage.</li><li>Regional spread: Reports said Iran also struck energy facilities in Saudi Arabia, raising the risk of a broader regional spillover.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran: Foreign minister Abbas Araghchi warned Tehran would show “zero restraint” if Iranian energy infrastructure was targeted again, casting the latest attacks as a response to the strike on South Pars. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/19/iran-says-it-will-show-zero-restraint-if-energy-infrastructure-is-targeted-again'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Kuwait: Authorities reported a refinery fire after drone strikes, showing the attacks had spread beyond gas export sites to oil-processing assets elsewhere in the Gulf. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/fire-at-kuwait-refinery-as-gulf-states-come-under-drone-missile-attacks/article70764352.ece'>The Hindu</a>, <a href='https://www.telegraph.co.uk/world-news/2026/03/19/iran-launches-new-strikes-kuwait-gas-prices-rise-35pc/'>The Telegraph</a>)</li><li>Susan Ziadeh: The former U.S. ambassador to Qatar said Gulf states see the attacks on petroleum facilities as a dangerous expansion of the war, one that threatens countries trying to avoid being drawn in directly. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/israeli-and-iranian-strikes-on-oil-and-gas-facilities-rattle-global-markets'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Financial Times analysis: FT reported that the strikes hit a state that has built influence in part through mediation, suggesting damage to Qatar’s gas system could also test its diplomatic role. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/25f1a787-29e1-40f3-afa1-f7edd00500f8'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/middleeast/qatar-natural-gas-attacks-ras-laffan.html'>Why Iran’s Attack on an Energy Hub in Qatar Spooked Investors</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-israel-iran-war-live-updates-iran-strikes-qatar-ras-laffan-lng-gulf-energy-sites-south-pars-oil-prices-strait-hormuz-iran-targets-gulf-energy-sites-11235957#publisher=newsstand'>Israel-Iran War LIVE: Drone Attack Causes Fire At Kuwait Oil Refinery</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/19/i